## 6. Pre-checks

1. Confirm source and destination loopbacks are reachable in the MPLS/TE routing context.
2. Confirm MPLS and RSVP are enabled on all intended transit links.
3. Confirm TE bandwidth values and existing reservations match the TED input.
4. Confirm no maintenance window, fiber work, or known degradation exists on the selected path.
5. Confirm service attachment/binding plan: VPLS, VPWS, L3VPN, autoroute, static route, or policy route.

## 7. Change steps

1. Apply explicit-path object on the tunnel head-end only.
2. Apply or update the RSVP-TE tunnel/LSP object.
3. Do not bind customer service traffic until the LSP is up and verified.
4. Bind service to tunnel using the platform-specific method.
5. Monitor RSVP, MPLS, interface utilization, and customer service counters.

## 8. Post-checks

1. LSP/tunnel is up.
2. RRO/ERO matches the expected path.
3. RSVP session is present and reservation bandwidth is visible.
4. Service forwarding uses the intended LSP.
5. Customer-facing QoS/CIR enforcement remains on the PE policy.

## 9. Rollback

1. Remove service binding from the new LSP/tunnel.
2. Disable or remove the new LSP/tunnel.
3. Remove explicit-path object if unused.
4. Confirm traffic returned to the previous path or previous service state.

